// A permit that fires, and grants nothing the set would not have granted anyway.
//
// The second permit is the interesting one. It is NOT vacuous -- give it an approval and its
// condition holds, so it fires. It is redundant: the first permit already allows every Trade
// unconditionally, so deleting the second changes no verdict in any session.
//
//     python tests/strands/vacuity.py tests/policies/redundant_permit.dw
//     ==> permit #2  action == Trade   REDUNDANT   deleting it changes no verdict in any session
//
// That distinction is the reason both questions are asked rather than one. A VACUOUS permit never
// fires at all and is a bug: whatever it was meant to allow is unreachable. A REDUNDANT permit
// works perfectly and is still worth deleting, because the next person has to read it and work out
// that it never decides anything.
//
// This shape arrives by accretion rather than by anyone writing it: a broad rule is added later
// "temporarily", and the careful gated rule underneath it quietly stops mattering.

permit (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"Trade",
    resource
);

permit (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"Trade",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 1000s Anchor::Action::"Approve"::request{ }
};
